    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f91af0f_7f93cf50",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/test_sql_upgrade.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 2611,
      "author": {
        "id": 15054
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-28T01:23:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For example: UniqueConstraint A, B, C.\nwe can insert a line: A1, B1, C1. Then if we insert a same line A1, B1, C1, DB will raise DBDuplicateEntry error.\n\nBut if one of A, B, C columns is None, then the UniqueConstraint will lose efficacy. It means that if we insert a line A1, None, C1, and insert a same line A1, None C1, DB will not raise error.\n\nSo in our case, \"region_id\" could be None. To solve the problem here are two way:\n1. give \"reigon_id\" a default value, such as empty string. But \"regiond_id\" here has the FK in \"region\" table, so we must create a default region in region table first.\n\n2. We can control it at the Manage layer to forbid inserting this kind of value.\n\nI think the second way is better. So that I add a function here: https://review.openstack.org/#/c/524082/16/keystone/limit/backends/sql.py L185\n\nP.S. Maybe there is a better way to use DB operation. But I don\u0027t know. If so, it will be the best way!",
      "parentUuid": "bf8cb3f7_6e517f0e",
